<section>
  <div class="wrap body-copy">
    <div class="eyebrow reveal">Executive Summary</div>
    <h3 class="reveal" style="margin-top:16px;">Why this case study?</h3>
    <p class="reveal">In March 2011, security company RSA — a firm whose entire business was authentication and identity protection — was breached through a phishing email. Not a sophisticated exploit chain, not a zero-day delivered over the network: a spreadsheet, an inbox, and one employee who trusted the wrong sender. RSA later went public with an unusually detailed account of how the attack unfolded, which makes it one of the best-documented spear phishing case studies available — and a useful lens for understanding why phishing remains effective even against organizations built to prevent it.</p>
    <div class="pull-quote reveal">The strongest authentication technology in the world didn't stop the attack. A believable email did the damage before any of it mattered.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider"><span class="divider-node"></span></div>

<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head reveal">
      <div class="eyebrow">The Lure</div>
      <h2 class="display">What the email actually looked like.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body-copy reveal" style="margin-bottom:30px;">
      <p>The email itself was deliberately unremarkable. It carried the subject line <strong>"2011 Recruitment Plan"</strong> and was sent to two small groups of employees who weren't particularly high-value targets — a detail that mattered, since lower-profile inboxes tend to get less scrutiny. One version was reportedly styled to look like it came from a recruiting site. The message body was short: a brief note asking the recipient to review the attached spreadsheet.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="mail-mock reveal">
      <div class="mail-head"><span>From: HR Recruiting &lt;recruiting@[external-domain]&gt;</span><span>Mar 2011</span></div>
      <div class="mail-body">
        <div class="subj">2011 Recruitment Plan</div>
        <p>I forward this file to you for review. Please open and view it.</p>
        <div class="mail-attach">
          <svg width="15" height="15" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M14 2H6a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v16a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h12a2 2 0 0 0 2-2V8z"/><path d="M14 2v6h6"/></svg>
          2011 Recruitment plan.xls
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size:12px; color:var(--text-faint); margin-top:14px; max-width:620px;">Reconstructed for illustration based on public reporting of the email's subject line and contents — not the original file.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider"><span class="divider-node"></span></div>

<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head reveal">
      <div class="eyebrow">The Breakdown</div>
      <h2 class="display">Five stages, one afternoon.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="stage-list">
      <div class="stage-item reveal">
        <span class="stage-tag">Stage 01 — Reconnaissance</span>
        <h3>Picking low-value, low-scrutiny targets</h3>
        <p>Rather than targeting executives, the attackers picked small groups of ordinary employees — likely identified through open-source research such as social media and public staff listings. Lower-profile targets are less likely to be trained on high-alert phishing awareness and less likely to have their inboxes independently monitored.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stage-item reveal">
        <span class="stage-tag">Stage 02 — Delivery</span>
        <h3>A believable pretext, sent twice</h3>
        <p>Two phishing emails went out over a two-day period, each with the "2011 Recruitment Plan" subject line and an attached Excel file. The pretext was mundane by design — recruiting spreadsheets are exactly the kind of file an employee might expect to receive and open without a second thought.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stage-item reveal">
        <span class="stage-tag">Stage 03 — Execution</span>
        <h3>Retrieved from spam, opened anyway</h3>
        <p>At least one of the emails was initially caught by a junk-mail filter — and an employee retrieved it anyway and opened the attachment. That single action was enough: the spreadsheet contained a hidden Adobe Flash object exploiting an unpatched vulnerability, which silently installed a backdoor the moment the file was opened.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stage-item reveal">
        <span class="stage-tag">Stage 04 — Foothold</span>
        <h3>A quiet, reverse-connecting backdoor</h3>
        <p>The exploit installed a remote-access tool configured in "reverse-connect" mode — meaning the infected machine reached out to the attacker's server, rather than the attacker connecting inward. That direction of traffic is much harder for network defenses to flag, since it looks more like normal outbound activity than an intrusion.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stage-item reveal">
        <span class="stage-tag">Stage 05 — Escalation</span>
        <h3>From one inbox to the crown jewels</h3>
        <p>From that initial foothold, the attackers moved laterally through RSA's network, harvesting credentials and escalating privileges until they reached systems tied to the SecurID product itself — ultimately forcing RSA to work with customers worldwide on mitigating the exposure of its core authentication technology.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider"><span class="divider-node"></span></div>

<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head reveal">
      <div class="eyebrow">Takeaways</div>
      <h2 class="display">Why this still matters.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="finding-list">
      <div class="finding-item reveal"><span class="dot"></span><p><strong>Technical sophistication wasn't the weak point.</strong> The vulnerability was real, but the entire chain started with ordinary social engineering — a believable subject line and a file type nobody thinks twice about opening.</p></div>
      <div class="finding-item reveal"><span class="dot"></span><p><strong>Spam filters are a layer, not a guarantee.</strong> The email was caught by a junk filter and still succeeded, because a human being deliberately retrieved and opened it anyway.</p></div>
      <div class="finding-item reveal"><span class="dot"></span><p><strong>Low-value targets are often the real target.</strong> Attackers didn't go after executives first — they went after whoever was least likely to be suspicious, then worked their way toward what actually mattered.</p></div>
      <div class="finding-item reveal"><span class="dot"></span><p><strong>One click was the entire attack surface.</strong> Every later stage — the backdoor, the lateral movement, the eventual exposure of SecurID — depended on a single successful open of a single attachment.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

